generic_control_command_failed.description:
RainPoint rejected a control command sent through generic control.

- **Model:** {model}
- **Error:** {error}

Generic control uses an unverified mapping from RainPoint's product catalog rather than a tested per-model decoder, so this device's behavior is not fully proven.

As far as this integration can tell, the device's state was not changed by the failed command.

If this keeps happening, you can turn generic control off in the integration's options.

device_not_reporting.description:
RainPoint lists this device on your account, but the cloud has returned no readings for it across the last {missed_polls} checks, which run a couple of minutes apart.

- **Model:** {model}
- **Address:** {address}
- **Hub:** {hub_name}

This can happen when a device is paired over Bluetooth only, is out of range, or is switched off. The integration keeps checking and will clear this notice on its own once readings resume.

If this is a Bluetooth device, check whether it can also pair to a RainPoint hub. A Bluetooth-only device reports nothing to the cloud, so there is nothing for this integration to read; paired to a hub, the same device usually reports its readings and you will see far more of it here. Opening and closing it from Home Assistant may still be unavailable depending on the model, though that may come in a future release.

A **Not Reporting** entity was added for this device at the same time as this notice. You will find it on the device's own page under Diagnostic, and its attributes carry a pre-filled report link if you believe the device should be reporting.

confirm.description:
RainPoint has stopped listing this device on its hub, and has not listed it across the last {missed_polls} checks, which run a couple of minutes apart.

- **Device:** {device_name}
- **Model:** {model}
- **Address:** {address}
- **Hub:** {hub_name}
- **Entities left over:** {entity_count}

Those entities can no longer update, and nothing will make them available again.

This usually means the device was unpaired, or that RainPoint moved it to a different parent record on its side. If it moved, a fresh set of entities for the same hardware already exists alongside these leftover ones, so you may be seeing each reading twice.

Select **Submit** to remove the leftover entities, along with the history recorded against them. That cannot be undone.

RainPoint still lists this device on your account and it is reporting normally, but nothing has updated {entity_count} of its entities across the last {missed_polls} times this integration looked. How much time that covers depends on how often your devices send new readings, so it can be a good deal shorter than it sounds.

- **Device:** {device_name}
- **Model:** {model}
- **Address:** {address}
- **Hub:** {hub_name}
- **Entities that would be removed:** {entity_count}
{entity_list}

This usually means the device once reported something it no longer sends, or that a newer version of this integration replaced these entities with better ones.

One thing to check before you confirm: a reading that simply has not arrived since the last restart looks the same from here, and it will come back on its own once the device sends it again. If that is what this is, select Cancel and leave it alone. Watering zones are never offered here, so a zone you have not used yet is safe either way.

Select **Submit** to remove these entities, along with the history recorded against them. That cannot be undone.
